        "content": "<p>I'm planning to formalise the Schur–Cohn step for polynomials with <strong>complex</strong> coefficients, stated with <code>Polynomial</code>, and would like advice before writing Lean. The main question is whether mathlib wants this and, if so, where it should go.</p>\n<p>Statement. For <code>p : ℂ[X]</code> with <code>p.natDegree = n</code>, <code>1 ≤ n</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Polynomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ComplexConjugate</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- conjugate reciprocal at a fixed formal degree (not `reverse`, which reflects at natDegree)</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">conjRecip</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p.map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">starRingEnd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">reflect</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsStable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p.IsRoot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"n\">z</span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">schur</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">conj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p.coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p.coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">conjRecip</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">divX</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">isStable_iff_schur</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p.natDegree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">IsStable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"n\">p.coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"n\">p.coeff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsStable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">schur</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The proof is algebraic once <code>IsAlgClosed ℂ</code> is available. It uses the Blaschke identity, the factorisation <code>Splits.eval_eq_prod_roots</code>, and <code>(‖aₙ‖² − ‖a₀‖²) • p = aₙ X q + a₀ q*</code>, with <code>q*</code> taken at degree <code>n − 1</code>. It uses no continuity of roots and no contour integrals. Iterating gives a structurally recursive <code>Bool</code> test on lists of Gaussian integers/rationals, which I'd like to prove correct and run with <code>decide</code>.</p>\n<p>For the real case, S. Kamaguchi's <code>ar456-stationarity-lean</code> proves the step-down <code>isStationary_iff_stepDown_gen</code> for real AR coefficients of every degree, by a continuity/openness argument. The complex theorem implies its statement, and I would cite that repository. I have also asked the author directly whether they plan to upstream it (<a href=\"https://github.com/S-Kamaguchi/ar456-stationarity-lean/issues/1\">https://github.com/S-Kamaguchi/ar456-stationarity-lean/issues/1</a>).</p>\n<p>Status: only the Blaschke identity is formalised so far; the rest is a blueprint (<a href=\"https://github.com/kostadinstoyanovuk/schur-cohn/tree/main/blueprint\">https://github.com/kostadinstoyanovuk/schur-cohn/tree/main/blueprint</a>).</p>\n<p>Questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would mathlib want the general (complex) theorem, or is it considered out of scope?</li>\n<li>Where would it live? Options I can see: <code>Mathlib/Algebra/Polynomial/…</code> for the conjugate-reciprocal API, and <code>Mathlib/Analysis/Complex/Polynomial/…</code> for the stability theorem.</li>\n<li>Naming. Is there a preferred name for the \"conjugate, then <code>reflect n</code>\" operation (for example <code>Polynomial.conjReflect</code>)? Is there an existing convention for the unit-disc stability predicate, such as <code>Schur stable</code> in contrast to <code>Hurwitz stable</code>?</li>\n<li>Does anyone know of existing or in-progress work on Schur–Cohn, Jury, Routh–Hurwitz or Rouché in Lean that this should build on or avoid duplicating?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Thanks!</p>",
